  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-yone",
  "connector": "よ・ね",
  "gloss": "endings: you know, isn’t it, right?",
  "title": "よ・ね・よね — telling, sharing, checking",
  "pattern": "sentence + よ (I’m telling you) / ね (we share this) / よね (right?)",
  "explanation": "These little endings carry the feeling of a conversation. よ tells someone something they may not know: これ、おいしいよ — this is good, you know. ね shares a feeling, or checks you agree: いい{天気|てんき}ですね — lovely day, isn’t it. よね checks something you’re fairly sure of: {明日|あした}、{休|やす}みだよね？ — tomorrow’s a day off, right?",
  "natural": "ね is the glue of small talk; そうですね (“that’s true”) is the classic reply.",
  "stiff": "A strong よ to someone above you can sound like a correction. And ね on something only you know, like your own name, sounds odd.",
  "examples": [
